00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.600
Lunlet sits beside your Mac's notch while your coding agents work.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.800
Each vine strand shows one agent's weekly allowance left.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.800
When a turn starts or finishes, a short island grows from the notch.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.000
Open the panel right after, and it shows that conversation.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.600
Hover the limits for the weekly trend, an estimate range and daily usage.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.700
The Overview keeps each agent's limits apart and adds up recorded usage.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.000
Hover a day for each agent's tokens. Cost is an API-price estimate, never a bill.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.400
Claude Code shows its session and weekly limits, today's tokens and its sessions.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.200
Click anywhere else, and it folds back into the notch.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.400
Send the next prompt, and the island shows the new turn has started.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.500
Lock your Mac and step away while the turn keeps running.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.500
When it finishes, Lunlet sends a short notice to your own Feishu, Slack or Telegram bot.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.200
The notice names the project, the agent and the session, with a summary and a reply box.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.300
One reply from your phone continues that same session. Approvals always stay on your Mac.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.000
Lunlet — A little closer to your flow
